{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1782351174327,"sessionID":"ses_1039ff019ffe12Z8126LKUxZU8","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"todowrite","callID":"todowrite_24","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"todos":[{"content":"Design project structure and dependencies","priority":"high","status":"completed"},{"content":"Implement data loader and normalization","priority":"high","status":"in_progress"},{"content":"Implement query functions (matches, teams, players, competitions, stats)","priority":"high","status":"pending"},{"content":"Implement MCP server with FastMCP tools","priority":"high","status":"pending"},{"content":"Write tests covering required behaviors","priority":"high","status":"pending"},{"content":"Run tests and fix any issues","priority":"high","status":"pending"},{"content":"Verify build/install and server starts","priority":"medium","status":"pending"}]},"output":"[\n  {\n    \"content\": \"Design project structure and dependencies\",\n    \"status\": \"completed\",\n    \"priority\": \"high\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"content\": \"Implement data loader and normalization\",\n    \"status\": \"in_progress\",\n    \"priority\": \"high\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"content\": \"Implement query functions (matches, teams, players, competitions, stats)\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"priority\": \"high\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"content\": \"Implement MCP server with FastMCP tools\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"priority\": \"high\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"content\": \"Write tests covering required behaviors\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"priority\": \"high\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"content\": \"Run tests and fix any issues\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"priority\": \"high\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"content\": \"Verify build/install and server starts\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"priority\": \"medium\"\n  }\n]","metadata":{"todos":[{"content":"Design project structure and dependencies","status":"completed","priority":"high"},{"content":"Implement data loader and normalization","status":"in_progress","priority":"high"},{"content":"Implement query functions (matches, teams, players, competitions, stats)","status":"pending","priority":"high"},{"content":"Implement MCP server with FastMCP tools","status":"pending","priority":"high"},{"content":"Write tests covering required behaviors","status":"pending","priority":"high"},{"content":"Run tests and fix any issues","status":"pending","priority":"high"},{"content":"Verify build/install and server starts","status":"pending","priority":"medium"}],"truncated":false},"title":"6 todos","time":{"start":1782351174319,"end":1782351174322}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"FastMCP has `tool` decorator and `run` method. We'll do:\n\n```\nmcp = FastMCP(\"brazilian-soccer-mcp\", instructions=...)\n\n@mcp.tool()\ndef search_matches(...): ...\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    mcp.run(transport=\"stdio\")\n```\n\nNeed know signature of tool handlers. FastMCP tool functions are plain functions. They can return string; the framework wraps. Good.\n\nNeed ensure that data loading doesn't happen at import time? If server imported for tests, loading at module level could be slow but okay. But for tests, we can load lazily via a function; module-level might be okay (2 seconds). The benchmark expects <2 sec lookups; caching at import is fine.\n\nBetter create a singleton `SoccerKnowledgeGraph` class instantiated lazily via `_kg = None` and `get_kg()`. Server tools call `get_kg()`.\n\nNow let's implement knowledge_graph.py. Need careful with pandas nullable ints. Use `astype('Int64')`.\n\nLet's design `SoccerKnowledgeGraph`:\n- `__init__(self, data_dir)`: load all CSVs into `self.matches` DataFrame and `self.players` DataFrame.\n- `self.matches` columns: competition, season, date, home_team_raw, away_team_raw, home_team, away_team, home_goal, away_goal, round, stage, stadium, home_state, away_state, source, display strings.\n- `self.players` columns: ID, Name, Age, Nationality, Overall, Potential, Club, Position, Jersey Number, Height, Weight.\n\nTeam canonical functions as module-level with dictionaries.\n\nLet's write code. Need use absolute paths for data dir default relative to file. We can compute `DATA_DIR = Path(__file__).parent.parent / \"data\" / \"kaggle\"`. If package is `brazilian_soccer_mcp`, then parent is project root. Good.\n\nNow write. But first maybe test a snippet. We'll implement file by file. Let's create `brazilian_soccer_mcp/__init__.py`, `knowledge_graph.py`, `server.py`, `__main__.py`, `pyproject.toml`, `tests/test_kg.py`. Also maybe `README.md` not needed.\n\nPotential issue: Loading pandas reads all files each import; tests will import. Should be okay.\n\nNeed ensure functions handle missing season (NaN) by convert to nullable Int64; filtering by season `==` works with nullable? Use `self.matches['season'].notna() & (self.matches['season']==season)` maybe with `pd.isna`. Since nullable Int64 supports equality but returns NA; use `.fillna(False)`. We'll implement helper.\n\nNeed date filtering: parse input date string to date.\n\nCompetition matching: \n```\ndef _competition_match(query):\n    q = normalize(query)\n    if not q: return None\n    if 'libertadores' in q: return 'Copa Libertadores'\n    if 'brasileir' in q or q == 'serie a' or q == 'serie b' or q == 'serie c' or 'campeonato brasileiro' in q:\n        if 'serie b' in q: return 'Brasileirão Série B'\n        if 'serie c' in q: return 'Brasileirão Série C'\n        if q == 'serie a' or 'brasileir' in q or 'campeonato brasileiro' in q: return 'Brasileirão'\n    if 'copa do brasil' in q or 'brazilian cup' in q: return 'Copa do Brasil'\n    return None\n```\nBut we also need rows with competition column values. We'll store competition keys as above.\n\nFor `normalize_competition` maybe returns list of keys if \"Campeonato Brasileiro\" includes Serie A/B/C. We'll handle.\n\nNow implement code. Use type hints.\n\nNeed careful with dates. We will create date from parsed. For columns `datetime`, use `pd.to_datetime` then `.dt.date`. For BR-Football `date` similar. For novo `Data` with dayfirst.\n\nAlso `season` for BR-Football can be derived from date year if missing? It has no season column. We'll derive from date.year. For others use existing season.\n\n`round` for Libertadores stage; store in `round_or_stage`? We'll keep both stage and round. Display: if stage, show stage; else round.\n\n`stadium` only novo has Arena. Others null.\n\nPlayer data: `fifa_data.csv`. We'll load with low_memory=False maybe. Keep `Name`, `Age`, `Nationality`, `Overall`, `Potential`, `Club`, `Position`, `Jersey Number`, `Height`, `Weight`. Rename to lower columns. `Overall` numeric.\n\nSearch players by name substring (case-insensitive normalized), nationality substring, club substring, position substring, min_overall. Return sorted by Overall desc.\n\nNeed ensure UTF-8 reading. pandas default handles.\n\nNow let's write `knowledge_graph.py`. We'll use `unidecode`? Not installed. Use unicodedata. We'll write helper.\n\nPotential issue with `normalize_text` removing accents from \"São Paulo\" -> \"sao paulo\". Good.\n\nState extraction: regex patterns. Need also capture state from strings like \"Flamengo-RJ\" and \"Flamengo - RJ\". Also strings like \"Fluminense RJ\" and \"Vasco Da Gama RJ\". Also \"Barcelona-EQU\" parentheses? Actually \"Barcelona-EQU\" not state. We should only capture two-letter uppercase state codes? Barcelona-EQU is three letters. Use pattern `[- ]+([A-Z]{2})$` to capture two-letter. For \"Fluminense RJ\" pattern space + two letters. For \"Barcelona-EQU\" would capture \"QU\"? Bad. We can restrict to known Brazilian states? But Libertadores includes \"Nacional (URU)\", \"Barcelona-EQU\". We don't want to treat EQU as state. Use list of Brazilian state codes. `BR_STATES = {'AC','AL','AP','AM','BA','CE','DF','ES','GO','MA','MT','MS','MG','PA','PB','PR','PE','PI','RJ','RN','RS','RO','RR','SC','SP','SE','TO'}`. Extract only if captured code in BR_STATES. Good.\n\nFor \"Barcelona-EQU\", code 'QU' not in list -> state None. For \"Nacional (URU)\", parenthetical capture URU not in list -> None. Good.\n\nAlso some team names like \"4 de Julho - PI\" -> PI. Good.\n\nPrefix stripping: Need be careful not to strip prefixes that are part of club name? For \"Sport Club do Recife\", stripping \"sport club\" leaves \"do recife\". But alias for \"Sport\" maybe \"sport\" and \"sport club do recife\". We'll include alias. But if we strip prefix, base becomes \"do recife\" which doesn't match. So maybe strip a specific set only when resulting base still meaningful? We can use alias matching on raw base before prefix stripping too. In canonical function, we can check both raw normalized base and cleaned base against aliases. For Corinthians, raw base \"sport club corinthians paulista\" matches alias exactly. For Flamengo full, raw base \"clube de regatas do flamengo\" does not match alias; cleaned base \"flamengo\" matches. So try both.\n\nLet's define `clean_base(base)`:\n```\nfor prefix in PREFIXES:\n    if nb.startswith(prefix + ' '):\n        nb = nb[len(prefix)+1:]\n```\nBut order longest prefix first. PREFIXES list sorted by length desc.\n\nThen `get_canonical(name)`:\n```\nraw_base, state = extract_team(name)\nnb_raw = normalize(raw_base)\nnb_clean = clean_base(nb_raw)\n# exact alias match with state check\nfor nb in (nb_clean, nb_raw):\n    for key, info in CANONICAL.items():\n        if nb in info['aliases']:\n            if state is None or state in info['states']:\n                return key\n# atletico abbreviation disambiguation\nif nb_clean in ('atletico','athletico'):\n    if state == 'PR': return 'athletico-pr'\n    if state == 'MG': return 'atletico-mg'\n    if state == 'GO': return 'atletico-go'\n# prefix match\nfor nb in (nb_clean, nb_raw):\n    for key, info in CANONICAL.items():\n        for alias in info['aliases']:\n            if nb.startswith(alias + ' '):\n                if state is None or state in info['states']:\n                    return key\nreturn nb_clean\n```\nBut this would canonicalize \"Flamengo do Piauí\" state PI with alias \"flamengo\" prefix -> state not in states -> skip. Then return nb_clean \"flamengo do piaui\". Good.\n\nFor \"Corinthians Paulista\" cleaned base = \"corinthians paulista\" alias exact -> canonical. Good.\n\nFor \"Athletico Paranaense\" cleaned base = \"athletico paranaense\" alias exact -> canonical \"athletico-pr\". Good.\n\nFor \"Atletico Mineiro\" cleaned base = \"atletico mineiro\" alias exact -> canonical \"atletico-mg\". Good.\n\nFor \"Atletico-PR\" (cup) raw base \"Atletico\", state PR -> abbreviation rule returns \"athletico-pr\". But note query \"Atletico\" without state should maybe match all Atletico. We keep query canonical as clean base \"atletico\" and team matches by prefix. We'll design `team_matches(raw, query)` using canonical keys and prefix.\n\n`team_matches(raw_name, query_name)`:\n```\nc1 = get_canonical(raw_name)\nq = get_canonical(query_name)\n# exact\nif c1 == q: return True\n# query is prefix of c1 (e.g. query \"atletico\" matches atletico-mg)\nif c1.startswith(q + '-'): return True  # for canonical keys with suffix\nif c1.startswith(q + ' '): return True\n# query with spaces: e.g., \"sao paulo\" matches \"sao paulo fc\"\nif (' ' + q + ' ') in (' ' + c1 + ' '): return True\nreturn False\n```\nBut for canonical key \"flamengo\" query \"flamengo\" exact. For \"flamengo do piaui\" query \"flamengo\" c1 startswith q+' ' -> would match. That's what we wanted to avoid. But get_canonical for Flamengo do Piauí returned its own key, not canonical \"flamengo\", because state mismatch. Good.\n\nFor \"Flamengo-RJ\" c1=\"flamengo\". Good.\n\nFor \"Athletico-PR\" canonical key \"athletico-pr\". Query \"Athletico\" q=\"athletico\". c1 startswith q+'-' -> match. Good. Query \"Atletico\" q=\"atletico\"; c1 starts \"athletico\"? No. Could add fuzzy: normalize 'h' difference? Maybe include canonical key aliases? We can add alias \"atletico\" to \"athletico-pr\"? But then query \"Atletico\" would also match Atletico-MG/GO because their keys start with \"atletico-\". That's okay maybe. But adding alias \"atletico\" to \"athletico-pr\" and \"atletico-mg\"/\"atletico-go\" all will cause query \"Atletico\" to match all four. If that's desired, fine. But then \"Atletico-MG\" alias includes \"atletico mineiro\" etc. Query \"Atletico\" with state None would match all via exact alias for each? Since multiple canonical teams have alias \"atletico\", the loop returns first in iteration order, not all. But `team_matches` after canonicalization would compare keys; query canonical would be first encountered key (say atletico-mg). That would only match that key. To match all Atletico clubs, we need query canonical to remain \"atletico\" and team canonical keys start with \"atletico-\". But \"athletico-pr\" key starts with \"athletico\" not \"atletico\". We can canonicalize Athletico-PR to key \"atletico-pr\" (without h) so query \"atletico\" prefix matches. Actually we can store canonical key for Athletico Paranaense as \"atletico-pr\". Then query \"Athletico\" normalizes to \"atletico\", matches. The display name remains \"Athletico-PR\". That's good. Similarly aliases normalized lose h. So all Atletico family keys start with \"atletico\". Then query \"Atletico\" matches all three. Query \"Athletico\" also normalizes to \"atletico\". Great.\n\nBut be careful: For \"Atletico-PR\" from cup spelling without h, it maps to same key. Good.\n\nSo canonical keys use normalized (accent/h) forms. Display names use proper spelling.\n\nFor team_matches, exact or prefix with '-' or ' '. For query \"Corinthians\" q=\"corinthians\", raw c1=\"corinthians\" exact. For query \"Santos\" exact. For query \"Sao Paulo\" q=\"sao paulo\", raw c1 \"sao paulo\" exact. Good.\n\nWhat about query \"Real Atlético\"? This is an Argentine team? Actually there is \"Real Atlético\" in Libertadores. It would not match \"Atletico\" because canonical key maybe \"real atletico\". It contains \"atletico\" as second word. We can allow if q appears as whole word in c1. Add condition: `if (' ' + q + ' ') in ' ' + c1 + ' ': return True`. For q \"atletico\" and c1 \"real atletico\" -> match. But that would also match \"flamengo atletico\" unlikely. Accept.\n\nBut for query \"Flamengo\", c1 \"flamengo do piaui\" contains whole word \"flamengo\" -> would match! Because we didn't assign canonical due to state mismatch; c1 = \"flamengo do piaui\"; contains whole word \"flamengo\". That reintroduces false positive. To avoid, for known big clubs, if state mismatches we should not allow whole-word match? The issue is that c1 retains the base. We could assign c1 to None or to own base; then contains would match. So we need an additional check: if query is a known canonical alias and the raw state is known and differs from expected states, exclude. We can implement `team_matches` to first check state exclusion.\n\nSimplify: In `team_matches`:\n```\nraw_canon = get_canonical(raw_name)\nq_canon = get_canonical(query_name)\nraw_base, raw_state = extract_team(raw_name)\nq_clean = clean_base(normalize(raw_base? query?))\n# state guard: if q_clean corresponds to a known big club with expected states and raw_state not in expected states and raw_state is not None: return False\n# Then do matching.\n```\nBut how to know q_clean expected states? We have CANONICAL dict. If query canonical key is a known key, get expected states. If raw_state present and not in expected states, return False. But if raw_state is None (Libertadores no state), include. However for Flamengo do Piauí raw_state PI is not None and not expected RJ -> exclude. For Fluminense de Feira BA -> exclude. For Grêmio Barueri SP -> exclude. Good.\n\nWhat about \"America-MG\" query \"America\" (not in our big clubs maybe) raw_state MG; expected states not defined; include. Good.\n\nWhat about \"Atletico Paranaense\" from BR-Football no state; raw_state None; query \"Atletico\" would match. Good.\n\nWhat about \"Flamengo\" in Libertadores no state; query \"Flamengo\" matches. Good.\n\nState guard function:\n```\ndef _state_guard(raw_state, query_canonical_key):\n    if raw_state is None or query_canonical_key not in CANONICAL:\n        return True\n    return raw_state in CANONICAL[query_canonical_key]['states']\n```\nBut query canonical key may be e.g., \"atletico\" (not a key) for generic query; guard not applied. That's okay; generic query includes all variants.\n\nBut for query \"Flamengo\", q canonical key = \"flamengo\" (key). Guard uses expected states {RJ}. Raw \"Flamengo do Piauí\" state PI -> exclude. Good. Raw \"Flamengo-RJ\" state RJ -> include. Raw \"Flamengo\" state None -> include.\n\nFor query \"Corinthians\", q key \"corinthians\" states {SP}. Raw \"Corinthians-SP\" include. Raw \"Corinthians\" state None include. Good.\n\nFor query \"São Paulo\", q key \"sao-paulo\" states {SP}. Raw \"São Paulo - SP\" include. Good.\n\nNeed handle query canonical key extraction for abbreviations: query \"Atletico-MG\" -> get_canonical returns \"atletico-mg\". Good. Query \"Athletico\" -> returns \"atletico-pr\" maybe because alias \"athletico\" maps to athletico-pr? Wait if we alias \"athletico\" to key \"atletico-pr\", then query \"Athletico\" canonicalizes to \"atletico-pr\". Then team_matches for Atletico-MG would not match. We don't want that. Better not include alias \"athletico\" alone; let query \"Athletico\" canonicalize to clean base \"atletico\" (no key). Then team_matches prefix matches \"atletico-mg\", \"atletico-go\", \"atletico-pr\". Good. But then full name \"Athletico Paranaense\" canonicalizes to key \"atletico-pr\" via alias. Query \"Athletico\" (alone) won't match that via exact; prefix \"atletico\" matches key \"atletico-pr\". Good. Query \"Atletico\" also prefix matches. So keep alias for full name only.\n\nBut our `get_canonical` exact alias loop includes alias \"athletico paranaense\" etc. Query \"Athletico\" clean base \"atletico\" won't match alias. Good. If user spells \"Athletico-PR\", clean base \"athletico\" state PR -> abbreviation rule returns key \"atletico-pr\". Good.\n\nBut what about raw name \"Athletico\" from Libertadores (no state) -> clean base \"athletico\", state None, abbreviation rule cannot decide -> returns \"atletico\". Then query \"Athletico\" q=\"atletico\" prefix matches. Good. Query \"Athletico-PR\" q=\"atletico-pr\" exact? raw canonical \"atletico\" not exact; prefix \"atletico-\"? c1 starts with \"atletico\" but not \"atletico-\". It is \"atletico\". Not match. Hmm. If user asks \"Athletico-PR\" expecting to include Libertadores \"Athletico\", that might be a mismatch. But acceptable; Libertadores uses \"Athletico\" generally means Athletico Paranaense. We could canonicalize raw \"Athletico\" (state None) to \"atletico-pr\" by default? But then query \"Atletico\" would match only Athletico-PR, not Atletico-MG. For Libertadores, only Athletico Paranaense appears. Could default to Athletico-PR when state None and base is \"athletico\" (because Athletico is only Athletico Paranaense). However Atletico-MG uses \"Atlético\" not \"Athletico\". Since spelling difference, maybe safe to map \"athletico\" to athletico-pr. But normalized we collapse accents, \"atletico\" ambiguous. Let's not overcomplicate. Most queries will be clear.\n\nNow implement `CANONICAL` with aliases normalized.\n\nLet's list canonical dict carefully. Use normalized keys:\n```\nCANONICAL = {\n    'flamengo': {'display': 'Flamengo', 'states': {'RJ'}, 'aliases': {'flamengo'}},\n    'fluminense': {'display': 'Fluminense', 'states': {'RJ'}, 'aliases': {'fluminense'}},\n    'palmeiras': {'display': 'Palmeiras', 'states': {'SP'}, 'aliases': {'palmeiras'}},\n    'corinthians': {'display': 'Corinthians', 'states': {'SP'}, 'aliases': {'corinthians', 'corinthians paulista', 'sport club corinthians paulista'}},\n    'sao-paulo': {'display': 'São Paulo', 'states': {'SP'}, 'aliases': {'sao paulo', 'sao paulo fc'}},\n    'santos': {'display': 'Santos', 'states': {'SP'}, 'aliases': {'santos'}},\n    'vasco': {'display': 'Vasco da Gama', 'states': {'RJ'}, 'aliases': {'vasco', 'vasco da gama'}},\n    'gremio': {'display': 'Grêmio', 'states': {'RS'}, 'aliases': {'gremio'}},\n    'atletico-mg': {'display': 'Atlético-MG', 'states': {'MG'}, 'aliases': {'atletico mineiro', 'atletico mg', 'atletico-mg'}},\n    'atletico-go': {'display': 'Atlético-GO', 'states': {'GO'}, 'aliases': {'atletico goianiense', 'atletico go', 'atletico-go'}},\n    'atletico-pr': {'display': 'Athletico-PR', 'states': {'PR'}, 'aliases': {'athletico', 'athletico paranaense', 'athletico pr', 'athletico-pr', 'atletico paranaense', 'atletico pr', 'atletico-pr'}},\n    # Note: alias 'athletico' alone not included; full names included.\n    'cruzeiro': {'display': 'Cruzeiro', 'states': {'MG'}, 'aliases': {'cruzeiro'}},\n    'botafogo': {'display': 'Botafogo', 'states': {'RJ'}, 'aliases': {'botafogo'}},\n    'bahia': {'display': 'Bahia', 'states': {'BA'}, 'aliases': {'bahia', 'esporte clube bahia'}},\n    'internacional': {'display': 'Internacional', 'states': {'RS'}, 'aliases': {'internacional'}},\n    'coritiba': {'display': 'Coritiba', 'states': {'PR'}, 'aliases': {'coritiba'}},\n    'fortaleza': {'display': 'Fortaleza', 'states': {'CE'}, 'aliases': {'fortaleza'}},\n    'ceara': {'display': 'Ceará', 'states': {'CE'}, 'aliases': {'ceara'}},\n    'goias': {'display': 'Goiás', 'states': {'GO'}, 'aliases': {'goias'}},\n    'avai': {'display': 'Avaí', 'states': {'SC'}, 'aliases': {'avai'}},\n    'chapecoense': {'display': 'Chapecoense', 'states': {'SC'}, 'aliases': {'chapecoense'}},\n    'sport': {'display': 'Sport', 'states': {'PE'}, 'aliases': {'sport', 'sport club do recife'}},\n    'ponte-preta': {'display': 'Ponte Preta', 'states': {'SP'}, 'aliases': {'ponte preta'}},\n}\n```\nNeed ensure aliases normalized. We normalize when building.\n\nPrefix stripping list:\n```\nPREFIXES = [\n    \"sport club do recife\",\n    \"sport club\",\n    \"sociedade esportiva\",\n    \"clube de regatas do\",\n    \"clube de regatas\",\n    \"esporte clube\",\n    \"futebol clube\",\n    \"clube\",\n    \"esporte\",\n    \"futebol\",\n    \"sociedade\",\n    \"sport\",\n]\n```\nSorted longest first.\n\nExtract state function:\n```\ndef extract_state(name):\n    # Remove parenthetical at end e.g. (URU)\n    s = name.strip()\n    # pattern: optional space/hyphen then two uppercase letters at end\n    m = re.search(r'(?:^|[\\s\\-])([A-Z]{2})$', s)\n    if m and m.group(1) in BR_STATES:\n        base = s[:m.start()].strip(' -')\n        return base, m.group(1)\n    # pattern for names like \"Fluminense RJ\" already no hyphen; above matches? [\\s\\-] before two uppercase at end; yes space works. But need ensure not match \"Nacional (URU)\"? The pattern expects end with two uppercase letters; Nacional (URU) ends with 'U' after ')'? Actually string ends with ')', so no match. Good. For \"Barcelona-EQU\" end with 'U', but group length 2 'QU' not in states. Good.\n    return s, None\n```\nBut `re.search` with `(?:^|[\\s\\-])([A-Z]{2})$` will also match \"ABC - RN\" group RN. Good. It will match \"4 de Julho - PI\" group PI. Good. For \"A.b.c. - RN\"? name has dots but group RN. Good.\n\nNeed also extract parenthetical country codes maybe. We'll just remove parentheses before state extraction? For \"Nacional (URU)\", state extraction fails (ends with ')'), returns base \"Nacional (URU)\". We can optionally remove parenthetical suffix: `re.sub(r'\\s*\\([^)]*\\)\\s*$', '', s)`. But that would remove \"(URU)\" from \"Nacional (URU)\", base \"Nacional\". Good. Also remove \" (antigo ... )\"? \"Boavista Sport Club (antigo Esporte Clube Barreira) - RJ\" -> remove parenthetical at end? The parenthetical is before state, so `re.sub` removes it -> \"Boavista Sport Club - RJ\". Good. Then state extraction works. But careful: some clubs may have parentheses part of name not suffix; rare. We'll remove suffix parentheses.\n\nDate parsing:\n```\ndef _parse_date_series(series, dayfirst=False):\n    dt = pd.to_datetime(series, errors='coerce', dayfirst=dayfirst)\n    return dt\n```\nThen `.dt.date` for date only. For columns with time, use datetime.\n\nSeason parsing:\n```\ndef _to_int(series):\n    return pd.to_numeric(series, errors='coerce').astype('Int64')\n```\n\nUnion DataFrame construction:\n```\nrows = []\n# for each file, build dicts\nrows.append(...)\nself.matches = pd.DataFrame(rows)\n```\nBut building list of dicts from pandas rows might be slower but 27k rows fine. Could use pd.concat.\n\nLet's implement per file:\n- Brasileirao: df['date'] = pd.to_datetime(df['datetime'], errors='coerce').dt.date; season = _to_int(df['season']); home/away raw; round = df['round']; competition='Brasileirão'.\n- Cup: same; competition='Copa do Brasil'; round = df['round'].\n- Libertadores: date; season; stage=df['stage']; competition='Copa Libertadores'; round maybe None.\n- BR-Football: date from df['date']; season from date.year; competition from tournament mapping; round None; stage None; extra stats maybe not needed but we can include.\n- novo: date from df['Data'] dayfirst; season from df['Ano']; round from df['Rodada']; competition='Brasileirão'; stadium=df['Arena']; home/away raw columns.\n\nNote: BR-Football includes Serie B/C; for competition mapping, store 'Brasileirão Série B' etc. For query 'Brasileirão', filter only 'Brasileirão'. For query 'Campeonato Brasileiro', include all 'Brasileirão*'.\n\nNeed sort matches by date desc for display.\n\nFor standings, use league table. Need decide if include all matches or per competition/season. For Copa/Libertadores, points still can be computed. For cup with knockout, wins/losses. But standings maybe not meaningful. We can still calculate.\n\nFor team stats, compute home/away/both. Need venue param.\n\nNeed functions return strings. Let's write formatting. Use `\\n`.\n\nPotential performance: all pandas operations on ~27k rows are fast.\n\nNow let's implement knowledge_graph.py. Use Write tool. Need read not needed for new files. Use write.\n\nBefore writing, update todo.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_efc6891e60010N3iyW9ZQkyGAV","sessionID":"ses_1039ff019ffe12Z8126LKUxZU8","messageID":"msg_efc66bc85001wi4YLLp7ubQ4Cs"}}
